2. **Critical Fluvial Velocity Thresholds**:
   - **$15\text{ cm/s}$ ($0.15\text{ m/s}$)**: Minimum threshold required to entrain and transport **fine sand** ($0.05\text{--}0.25\text{ mm}$);
   - **$30\text{ cm/s}$ ($0.30\text{ m/s}$)**: Threshold velocity required to roll and transport **fine gravel** ($2\text{--}4\text{ mm}$);
   - **$1.2\text{ m/s}$**: Turbulent threshold required to mobilize **stones and cobbles** ($64\text{--}256\text{ mm}$);
   - **$9.0\text{ m/s}$**: Extreme torrential flood velocity capable of rolling and transporting **massive, multi-tonne boulders** ($>1\text{ meter}$ diameter).

3. **Step 2: Subdivision into Coarse Gravel / Sand ($1\text{ cm}^3$ Cubes)**:
   - Subdividing the $1\text{ m}^3$ cube into cubes of edge length $l = 1\text{ cm} = 0.01\text{ m}$:
   - Number of particles produced: $N = (100)^3 = 1,000,000$ ($10^6$) cubes;
   - Surface area per cube: $a = 6 \times (0.01\text{ m})^2 = 0.0006\text{ m}^2$;
   - Total Surface Area: $A_{\text{total}} = 10^6 \times 0.0006\text{ m}^2 = \mathbf{600\text{ m}^2}$ (**a $100\text{-fold}$ expansion**).

4. **Step 3: Pulverization to Colloidal Clay ($1\text{ }\mu\text{m}$ Particles)**:
   - Further pulverizing the $1\text{ m}^3$ cube down to colloidal clay particles of edge length $l = 1\text{ }\mu\text{m} = 10^{-6}\text{ m}$:
   - Number of particles produced: $N = (10^6)^3 = 10^{18}$ particles;
   - Surface area per particle: $a = 6 \times (10^{-6}\text{ m})^2 = 6 \times 10^{-12}\text{ m}^2$;
   - Total Surface Area: $A_{\text{total}} = 10^{18} \times (6 \times 10^{-12}\text{ m}^2) = \mathbf{6,000,000\text{ m}^2} = \mathbf{6\text{ km}^2}$ (**a $1,000,000\text{-fold}$ expansion**).
